# Test: `show firewall ruleset <table>` over the real SSH `ze cli` path names the
#       DNS name that supplied each address in a domain group's set.
# Spec: plan/spec-firewall-domain-group.md -- Wiring Test row 5, user story 3
# VALIDATES: the enrichment reaches an operator. handleShowFirewallRuleset
#            (internal/plugins/firewall/nft/cmd_show.go) calls show.Enrich, the
#            hub forwards `enrich-show` to the domain plugin, enrichShow
#            (internal/component/firewall/plugins/domain/enrich.go) attaches
#            source-name to every element it resolved, and the merged answer is
#            rendered to the client.
# PREVENTS: the enrichment being proven at the enricher alone. enrichShow
#           returning the right map says nothing about whether the registration
#           key matches the command string the show handler passes, whether the
#           merge survives the plugin boundary, or whether the name reaches the
#           client at all. This file is the only assertion made from outside the
#           daemon.
#
# RUN GREEN on 2026-09-06, and observed RED first: with enrichShow
# (internal/component/firewall/plugins/domain/enrich.go) answering nothingToAdd,
# the pattern at the end of this file fails and the two needles above it still
# pass, which is what says the pattern is the assertion that reads the
# enrichment. The command that runs it is `./le functional firewall`; on a host
# without CAP_NET_ADMIN and CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE it is skipped by the option
# below, and the runner that executes it in the gate is
# `./le qemu netns-test suites firewall`.
#
# The fixture is domainGroupShow
# (internal/test/fixture/netfilter_fixture_domain_group.go). In order it:
#   1. Waits for the daemon, waits for the SSH port, and provisions client
#      credentials with `ze init` into a sandbox config dir, the way
#      test/firewall/firewall-cli-show.ci's fixture does.
#   2. Serves DNS on 127.0.0.1:53 from the stub's default zone, which answers
#      web.example.test with A 203.0.113.10.
#   3. Runs `update firewall domain-group web | json`, so the group has data.
#      Its answer is not printed: it carries counts, and this file reads one
#      command's answer.
#   4. Reads the kernel back until the group's set holds the address, and prints
#      NOTHING while doing it. handleShowFirewallRuleset renders
#      firewall.LastApplied(), which is the daemon's own merged snapshot, so a
#      set that never reached nftables would render all the same. This read is
#      what makes the assertions below evidence about a live filter.
#   5. Runs `show firewall ruleset fw_dg | json` over `ze cli` and prints the
#      answer. The pipe is explicit because the assertions read quoted JSON
#      keys, and the daemon's default render format is whatever its
#      configuration says.
#
# The term matching the group is declared in the config below, not committed by
# the fixture. A daemon START reads its config through OnConfigure alone and
# runs no verify (configure, internal/component/firewall/plugins/domain/
# domain.go), so the refusal AC-6 describes belongs to a commit and not to a
# boot. What a boot does instead is hold the table back
# (dropTablesMissingAProvidedSet, internal/component/firewall/registry.go) until
# the update in step 3 resolves the group, which is the cold start this test
# walks through. `ze cli -c` runs ONE command and the config editor is a TUI, so
# committing a term mid-test would mean driving that editor over a pty; the
# config below reaches the same state with the product doing the same thing.
#
# ONE SCENARIO PER FILE. An expect=stdout: assertion is FILE-level: the runner
# checks it against the concatenated output of every command in the file
# (runner_exec.go, rec.ClientOutput), stdout and stderr together. Nothing here
# asserts an absence, so the whole file reads one command's answer.
#
# Auth setup mirrors test/firewall/firewall-cli-show.ci: the daemon config
# declares a user and an SSH server, and the fixture drives `ze cli` against it.
#
# This test programs nftables and binds port 53, so it needs CAP_NET_ADMIN and
# CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE, not merely Linux.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,net-bind
# Serialize against every other test that binds port 53. The port is NODE-WIDE
# and the address is fixed by the type of the `system name-server` leaf. One
# group covers the nftables contention too: the ruleset is node-wide and the set
# names come from the group name (setNames, sets.go). A test carries ONE
# exclusive group (Record.ExclusiveGroup, record_parse.go), so this has to be
# the one that covers both.
option=exclusive:group=dns-stub-port-53
# error, not the default and not the info level firewall-cli-show.ci uses.
# dropTablesMissingAProvidedSet (internal/component/firewall/registry.go) logs
# `set=domain_v4_web` at WARN every time this table is held back, and it is held
# back at boot until the update in step 3 resolves the group. The daemon's
# stderr lands in the same buffer the assertions below read (runner_exec.go,
# teeDaemonStderr), so that line would satisfy the set-name assertion on its own
# and leave it proving nothing about what the show handler rendered.
option=env:var=ze.log.firewall:value=error
# Each test gets its own persisted address cache. DefaultConfigDir
# (internal/core/paths/paths.go) otherwise resolves to the directory the binary
# sits in, which every test in the run shares, and a `web` group left cached by
# a sibling would let this daemon program the set at boot rather than at step 3.
option=env:var=ze.config.dir:value=.
option=timeout:value=30s

stdin=ze-fw:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	# The hub resolver reads NameServers[0] as its server (newResolvers,
	# cmd/ze/hub/main_system.go), so this points every lookup at the fixture's
	# stub rather than at the host's real DNS.
	name-server 127.0.0.1;
	dns {
		resolv-conf-path "";
	}
	authentication {
		user operator {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO";
		}
	}
}

firewall {
	backend nft;
	domain-group web {
		domain-names [ web.example.test ];
		ttl-floor 60;
	}
	table fw_dg {
		family inet;
		chain input {
			type filter;
			hook input;
			priority 0;
			policy accept;
			# A drop-policy chain would lock out the `ze cli` -> SSH connection
			# this test is driven over, so the policy is accept.
			term allow-loopback {
				from { input-interface lo; }
				then { accept; }
			}
			# The rule the enrichment is read from. It is what makes the plugin
			# register a set at all (buildTables, sets.go), and until the group
			# resolves it is what holds this whole table back.
			term allow-web {
				from { source-domain-group web; }
				then { accept; }
			}
		}
	}
}

environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true;
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			# $PORT2 is the second of the two ports the runner leases to this
			# test (TestPortSpan, internal/test/runner/ports.go), passed to the
			# fixture as its one argument.
			port $PORT2;
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-fw
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture firewall/firewall-cli-domain-group-show $PORT2:timeout=25s
expect=exit:code=0

# The set and its address are in the answer at all.
expect=stdout:contains=domain_v4_web
expect=stdout:contains=203.0.113.10

# The name is attached to THAT address, and not merely somewhere in the same
# document. `[^}]*` keeps both inside one element object, so a product that
# rendered the group's names in a separate list, or attached the name to the
# wrong element, fails this line while passing the two above. The alternation is
# there because the key order of a rendered map is not this test's business:
# source-name is the field enrichSet adds beside value (enrich.go), and either
# order says the same thing.
expect=stdout:pattern=(203\.0\.113\.10[^}]*web\.example\.test|web\.example\.test[^}]*203\.0\.113\.10)
